theory CompliantCurrency
begin

/*
================================================================================
  CURRENCY ISSUANCE AGAINST THE ISSUER'S OWN RECORD
================================================================================

  WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `compliant/`, not `wire-only/`.  Nothing here is
  checkable by a third party, and `infra-client-requirements.md` says so in
  the very obligation this theory models:

      "Issue only for the key you currently record.  A recovery adoption you
       have received overwrites your record of that subject (design 9.0.2),
       and after it you have nothing to issue for the old key and MUST NOT.
       NOBODY CAN CHECK THIS FOR YOU, and this is a commitment rather than an
       enforceable rule (design 1.1)."

  So the question here is NOT "can a relying party verify the issuer obeyed?"
  -- it cannot, and `wire-only/currency.spthy` is where the verifiable half
  lives.  The question is whether a node that DOES obey can still reach a
  state its own commitments forbid.  That is a coherence property of the
  written obligations, and it is worth checking because the obligations were
  written separately: design 12.6.5, design 9.0.2, and the infra-client rule
  above have never been read against one another by anything but a person.

  WHY THE RECORD IS PER-ISSUER, which is the whole shape of this model.
  design 12.6.5 does not promise that a rotation reaches every issuer.  It
  says the opposite, and prices it:

      "Issuance survives what replication does not.  A sibling issues a FRESH
       attestation reflecting whatever rotation reached the replicated state."

  So `Record` is indexed by ISSUER as well as subject.  A sibling that has not
  yet received the recovery adoption records the old key, and issuing for it
  is COMPLIANT -- it is issuing for the key it currently records.  The
  obligation binds each issuer to its own record and to nothing else.  Any
  model with a single global current-key fact would prove a stronger property
  than the design claims, and would be wrong.  `stale_sibling_issuance_is_
  reachable` below exists to keep that honest: it asserts the lagging trace is
  still THERE.  If a later edit makes that lemma falsify, the model has
  quietly started assuming instant replication.

  THE ABSTRACTION.  Signatures are gone.  `wire-only/currency.spthy` proves an
  accepted attestation is bound to its subject, key and epoch by a genuine
  signature; repeating that here would duplicate it and let the two copies
  drift.  This theory keeps only the STATE MACHINE, which is what the wire
  theory cannot see.
*/

builtins: multiset

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SETUP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

// An issuer begins with a record of some subject's key.  $I ranges over the
// patron and its stand-ins alike: design 12.6.5.1's ladder puts sibling,
// grandpatron and down-line issuers under the SAME obligation, each against
// its own replicated record, so one rule covers every rung.
/*  THE KEY IS THE SUBJECT'S, and several issuers record the same one -- the
    patron and the siblings that stand in for it (design 12.6.5.1).  Minting
    a fresh key per issuer would make each issuer's key its own and destroy
    the only interesting question here, which is what happens when a rotation
    reaches one issuer and not another.  `stale_sibling_issuance_is_reachable`
    falsified for exactly that reason before this was split out.            */
rule Subject_Key:
    [ Fr(~k) ]
  --[ SubjectKey($S, ~k) ]->
    [ !Key($S, ~k), Out(~k) ]

rule Initial_Record:
    [ !Key($S, k) ]
  --[ RecordOpened($I, $S, k), OpenedFor($I, $S), Installed($I, $S, k) ]->
    [ Record($I, $S, k) ]

/*  ONE RECORD PER SUBJECT, PER ISSUER, and the obligation is unstatable
    without it: infra-client says an adoption "overwrites YOUR RECORD of that
    subject" -- singular, one entry that a later adoption replaces.  An issuer
    holding two records for one subject could supersede one and keep issuing
    from the other while breaking no rule, because the rule is about "the key
    you currently record" and there would be no such thing.  Tamarin found
    exactly that trace when this restriction was absent.  It is a modelling
    artifact rather than a finding -- nothing in the design lets a node hold
    two current records for one subject -- but it is the kind that would have
    made the main lemma unprovable for a reason that looks like a defect.  */
restriction OneRecordPerSubject:
  "All I S #i #j. OpenedFor(I, S) @ #i & OpenedFor(I, S) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ISSUANCE -- the obligation itself
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  `Record` is LINEAR and put straight back.  That is deliberate: consuming it
  is what makes an issuance read the record at a point in time, and replacing
  it unchanged is what makes issuance not itself a state change.  Issuing does
  not alter what the issuer records; only an adoption does.
*/

rule Issue:
    [ Record($I, $S, k) ]
  --[ Issued($I, $S, k)
    , IssuedAgainstRecord($I, $S, k) ]->
    [ Record($I, $S, k), Out(<'currency', $S, k>) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SUPERSESSION -- a recovery adoption reaches THIS issuer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  design 9.0.2: the adoption overwrites this node's record of the subject.
  The old key is consumed and cannot come back -- there is no rule producing
  `Record($I,$S,kOld)` a second time, which is the model's way of saying the
  overwrite is not reversible by anything the issuer does.

  `AdoptionArrived` carries the issuer, because arrival is per-node.  A second
  issuer holding the same subject is untouched by this rule firing.
*/

rule Adoption_Arrives:
    [ Record($I, $S, kOld), Fr(~kNew) ]
  --[ AdoptionArrived($I, $S, kOld, ~kNew)   // see the scope note below
    , Superseded($I, $S, kOld)
    , Installed($I, $S, ~kNew) ]->
    [ Record($I, $S, ~kNew), Out(~kNew) ]

/*  A SCOPE NOTE ON THE SUCCESSOR.  The rule above mints `~kNew` per issuer,
    so in this model two issuers that both receive an adoption end up on
    different successors.  In the protocol they receive the SAME one.  The
    difference does not reach any lemma here -- the stale-sibling witness
    needs only the OLD key shared, which it is, and the
    names-the-new-key lemma concerns a single issuer -- but a later lemma
    comparing two issuers' successors would need this fixed first.        */
restriction Inequality:
  "All x y #i. NotEqual(x, y) @ #i ==> not(x = y)"

/*  A DEPENDENCY THIS MODEL FOUND, and the clearest thing it has produced.

    The rule above mints the successor with `Fr`, which is faithful:
    `wire-only/recovery.spthy` has the recovery meeting mint the successor,
    and design 9.1's successor is a key the subject has just generated.  But
    the first version of this theory took it from `In(kNew)`, and the main
    obligation was then FALSE, with a trace that is not an artifact:

      Both the initial key and every issued one reach the adversary, so it can
      hand back a key the issuer ALREADY SUPERSEDED as the new one.  k1 -> k2
      walks the record forward, k2 -> k1 walks it back, and the issuer then
      issues for k1 in full compliance with "issue only for the key you
      currently record" -- because it is once again the key it records.

    What forbids the walk-back is written in a DIFFERENT DOCUMENT, binding a
    DIFFERENT PARTY.  `light-client-requirements.md`: "Never take a series
    reissue into a series you have occupied before ... a counterparty holding
    that chain will reject a reissue that repeats a series in it."  The
    subject must not walk back, and a counterparty holding the chain can
    refuse -- so this half is not purely a commitment, which is why the infra
    node's obligation can lean on it.

    So the currency rule is coherent only in company.  Read alone it permits
    the trace above; read beside the light client's rule it does not.  That is
    what this tree is for, and it is invisible to `wire-only/`: an attestation
    issued after a walk-back is perfectly well formed, and no relying party
    can see the history.

    The rule below keeps the finding IN THE MODEL rather than only in this
    comment.  It is the non-compliant path, carved out by name in the lemmas.
    Deleting it would make the main obligation true for a reason the documents
    do not supply on their own.                                             */
rule Adoption_Arrives_Into_An_Abandoned_Line:
    [ Record($I, $S, kOld), In(kNew) ]
  --[ AdoptionArrived($I, $S, kOld, kNew)
    , Superseded($I, $S, kOld)
    , SeriesCheckSkipped($S)
    , NotEqual(kOld, kNew) ]->
    [ Record($I, $S, kNew) ]

/*  AND THE OTHER PARTY'S PROMISE, STATED AS AN AXIOM.  The restriction below
    removes every trace in which the subject walks back into a line it has
    occupied.  That is not this node's rule to keep -- it belongs to the
    light client, and a counterparty holding the chain can enforce it -- so
    the honest way to carry it is as a labelled assumption rather than as
    something this theory proves.  The same shape as
    `wire-only/currency.spthy`'s `NotExpiredBeforeAccept`.

    MUTATION TEST, and the reason the rule above is kept rather than deleted:
    comment out this restriction and
    `no_issuance_for_a_key_this_issuer_superseded` FALSIFIES, with the k1 ->
    k2 -> k1 trace described above.  The obligation is therefore not
    self-supporting, and the file records which other document holds it up. */
restriction TheSubjectDoesNotReturnToAnAbandonedLine:
  "All S #i. SeriesCheckSkipped(S) @ #i ==> F"

/*
================================================================================
  PROPERTIES
================================================================================
*/

/*  THE OBLIGATION, stated as the trace property it implies.  Once an adoption
    has overwritten this issuer's record of $S, this issuer never issues for
    the old key again.  Note both indices are $I: the claim is about ONE
    node's behaviour, which is the only thing an obligation can bind.       */
/*  TWO INVARIANTS THE PROVER NEEDS, and the reason it needs them.  `Issue`
    consumes `Record` and puts it straight back, so a backward search for
    "where did this record come from" regresses through unboundedly many
    issuances.  These two close the regress: a key enters a record by one of
    exactly two doors, and it enters once.                                  */
lemma a_key_enters_a_record_once [reuse]:
  all-traces
  "All I S k #a #b. Installed(I, S, k) @ #a & Installed(I, S, k) @ #b ==> #a = #b"

lemma a_record_key_entered_by_one_of_two_doors [reuse, use_induction]:
  all-traces
  "All I S k #j. IssuedAgainstRecord(I, S, k) @ #j
      ==> (Ex #o. RecordOpened(I, S, k) @ #o & #o < #j)
        | (Ex kOld #a. AdoptionArrived(I, S, kOld, k) @ #a & #a < #j)"

/*  ============================================================
    NOT DISCHARGED UNBOUNDED -- but verified over a bounded model
    ============================================================

    THE CLAIM, which is what this whole theory exists for:

      lemma no_issuance_for_a_key_this_issuer_superseded:
        all-traces
        "All I S k #i #j.
            Superseded(I, S, k) @ #i & Issued(I, S, k) @ #j
              ==> #j < #i"

    It is left commented out because TAMARIN DOES NOT DISCHARGE IT, and a
    lemma the gate never proves must not sit in the file looking proven.

    WHY.  `Issue` consumes `Record` and restores it, which is faithful --
    issuing does not change what a node records -- but it means the backward
    search for the origin of `Record($I,$S,k)` regresses through unboundedly
    many prior issuances.  The two invariants above were written to close that
    regress and both verify; the goal still does not follow from them inside
    420 seconds.

    WHAT WAS TRIED: `use_induction` on the goal; the negated form
    `not(Ex #j. ... & #i < #j)`; removing the `Out` facts; removing
    `OneRecordPerSubject`; removing the non-compliant rule entirely; and the
    two `[reuse]` invariants above.  None discharged it, and none of them
    FALSIFIED it either -- there is no counterexample, only no proof.

    WHAT IS ESTABLISHED, and it is more than belief.  `currency.bounded`
    appends a bound -- at most one supersession, at most two issuances -- and
    over that finite space the obligation VERIFIES, in 33 steps.  `run-all.sh`
    builds that file by appending the fragment to THIS one, so the bounded
    result is re-derived from these rules on every run and cannot drift from
    them.

    So the honest statement is: no counterexample exists within the bound,
    checked; the unbounded proof is what is missing.  Nothing else in this
    theory depends on it.

    A LONGER WALL CLOCK IS NOT THE ANSWER.  `run-all.sh` says a proof needing
    longer than the timeout needs a hint rather than more time, and that is
    right -- the regress is unbounded, so it does not terminate at any budget.
    What would close it is a different encoding of the record's lifetime, and
    that is a modelling decision rather than a tuning one.
    ============================================================  */

lemma issuance_after_supersession_names_the_new_key:
  exists-trace
  "Ex I S kOld kNew #a #b.
      AdoptionArrived(I, S, kOld, kNew) @ #a
    & Issued(I, S, kNew) @ #b
    & #a < #b"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY.  An issuance happens at all.                              */
lemma issuance_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex I S k #i. Issued(I, S, k) @ #i"

/*  THE LAG WITNESS, and the most important lemma in this file.

    Two issuers hold the same subject.  An adoption reaches the first and not
    the second, and the second then issues for the OLD key -- compliantly,
    because it is issuing for the key it currently records.  design 12.6.5
    says this trace exists and prices it ("issuance survives what replication
    does not").  Asserting it here means the model cannot quietly acquire an
    instant-replication assumption: strengthen `Adoption_Arrives` to touch
    every issuer and this lemma falsifies while every other lemma above stays
    green.                                                                  */
lemma stale_sibling_issuance_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex I1 I2 S k kNew #a #b.
      Superseded(I1, S, k) @ #a
    & AdoptionArrived(I1, S, k, kNew) @ #a
    & Issued(I2, S, k) @ #b
    & #a < #b
    & not(I1 = I2)"

end
